# A fractal bent by a fractal, which is stone. Warping one noise field by another
# buys flow: the veins run where they were pushed, not where the noise happens to.
# The folded output is drawn, because a crease is a vein. Three octaves on the warp
# and five on the veins: a warp made of fine detail pushes neighboring pixels
# opposite ways and tears the field. There is no sound in it.

description "A fractal bent by a fractal, which is stone."
tags "patterns"

requires flyback.picture

# The plane is pushed by how the first field reads at each point, so the second is
# read somewhere other than where it is drawn.
let push = fractal(z: t, scale: 0.9, octaves: "3")
let veins = warp(by: push, amount: 0.7)
  |> fractal(z: t, scale: 2.5, roughness: 0.55, octaves: "5")

# Backwards, so the creases are the dark veins and the body is the light stone; not
# all the way to black, since marble has no holes in it.
veins.folded |> remap(in_low: 0, in_high: 0.55, out_low: 1, out_high: 0.1)
  |> color.hsv(hue: push, saturation: 0.25, value: _)
  |> out.color
